# KNOWN GAP — durable repro for the STAGING-MOVE class at the SLICE write site.
#
# `Vector[String] s1 = v[a:b]` lowers to
#     _s = index_load _v, _range
#     [Mv] _dst = copy _s
#          move_zero _s          <- the staging site's own zero
#     ...  call @gorget_array_clone(copy _s)   <- a REAL later read of _s
# i.e. a move out of a source that is still read. It is benign TODAY only
# because the backend elides the zero when drop-tracking proves the read
# unobservable — which the MoveZero-as-kill liveness always concludes here.
# Core #8 forbids treating that as a pass.
#
# ⚠ The gap is the WRITE SITE, not this program's output: at HEAD this fixture
# builds clean and prints the right thing. The only instrument that can observe
# the defect is `GG_STAGING_MOVE_GUARD=fatal`, so the `#[ignore]`d test asserts
# the INTENDED state — zero `StagingMoveIntoOwnedSlot` violations under the
# promoter — which is RED at HEAD (`BUILD_RC=101`, 2 violations, first at
# `@main bb0 i23`) by construction. Graduate it (un-ignore, delete this copy,
# drop the ledger row in `tests/gaps/STAGING_MOVE_BURNDOWN.txt`, lower the
# lint's TRIP_CEILING) the round the slice write site routes through
# `LoweringContext::assign_with_move_follow_through`.
#
# A copy of the live `tests/fixtures/d22_slice_vector_string.gg` rather than a
# hand-minimised program: every smaller shape tried reduces to a DIFFERENT,
# already-fatal validator (`local _N read after MoveZero in bbK`), which is a
# false negative for this class.
#
# D22 colon-slice × Vector[String] (droppable element) — exercises the
# runtime `elem_clone` deep-clone branch in `gorget_array_slice`
# (`src/backend/c/runtime/runtime_array.c:475-479`), which never fires for
# `Vector[int]`. Core #12 element-type axis: the POD cell (Vector[int]) says
# nothing about the droppable-element cell.
void main():
    Vector[String] v = ["alpha", "beta", "gamma", "delta"]
    int a = 1
    int b = 3
    Vector[String] s1 = v[a:b]
    print(f"[a:b] {s1.get(0).unwrap()},{s1.get(1).unwrap()}")
    Vector[String] s2 = v[:]
    print(f"[:] len={s2.len()} {s2.get(0).unwrap()},{s2.get(3).unwrap()}")
    # Mutate the source AFTER the slice — the slice is an independent OWNED
    # value (D15 semantics), so the earlier snapshot survives.
    v.push("epsilon")
    print(f"after push {v.len()} s1[0]={s1.get(0).unwrap()}")
